{"name":"Sinkhole","hex":"#49716d","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sinkhole","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A hole formed in soluble rock by the action of water, serving to conduct surface water to an underground passage.","A depressed area in which waste or drainage collects."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To configure as a sinkhole (domain name server that gives non-routeable addresses)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sinkhole"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sinkhole","blocks":[{"text":"A sinkhole is a depression or hole in the ground caused by some form of collapse of the surface layer. The term is sometimes used to refer to doline, enclosed depressions that are also known as shakeholes, and to openings where surface water enters into underground passages known as ponor, swallow hole or swallet. A cenote is a type of sinkhole that exposes groundwater underneath. Sink and stream sink are more general terms for sites which drain surface water, possibly by infiltration into sediment or crumbled rock."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/07_Imotski_Crveno_Jezero_%281%29.jpg/330px-07_Imotski_Crveno_Jezero_%281%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sinkhole"},{"title":"Sinkhole (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Sinkhole (Korean: 싱크홀) is a 2021 South Korean disaster comedy film, directed by Kim Ji-hoon and starring Cha Seung-won, Kim Sung-kyun, Lee Kwang-soo and Kim Hye-jun. It is about an average man's house, that took 11 years to obtain, but going down a sinkhole 500 meters underground in a minute. The film with a production cost of ₩15 billion was slated to release in the Chuseok holidays of 2020 but its release was postponed due to the resurgence of the COVID-19 pandemic. It had its world premiere on August 6, at the 74th Locarno Film Festival held in Locarno, Switzerland. It was released in theatres on August 11, 2021."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/7/76/Sinkhole_2021_film.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sinkhole_(film)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Dead_Sea_sinkhole_by_David_Shankbone.jpg/500px-Dead_Sea_sinkhole_by_David_Shankbone.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Dead_Sea_sinkhole_by_David_Shankbone.jpg/1280px-Dead_Sea_sinkhole_by_David_Shankbone.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Dead_Sea_sinkhole_by_David_Shankbone.jpg/500px-Dead_Sea_sinkhole_by_David_Shankbone.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sinkholes near the Dead Sea, formed when underground salt is dissolved by freshwater intrusion, due to continuing sea-level drop.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADead_Sea_sinkhole_by_David_Shankbone.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Chinch%C3%B3n_dolina_c1991.jpg/500px-Chinch%C3%B3n_dolina_c1991.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Chinch%C3%B3n_dolina_c1991.jpg/1280px-Chinch%C3%B3n_dolina_c1991.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Chinch%C3%B3n_dolina_c1991.jpg/500px-Chinch%C3%B3n_dolina_c1991.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Collapse sinkhole in Chinchón, Spain.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChinch%C3%B3n_dolina_c1991.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Sinkhole.jpg/500px-Sinkhole.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Sinkhole.jpg/1280px-Sinkhole.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Sinkhole.jpg/500px-Sinkhole.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A 32-foot-deep (9.8-meter) collapse formed by rainwater leaking through pavement and carrying soil into a ruptured sewer pipe in the parking lot at Georgia Tech in Atlanta, Georgia in 2005","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASinkhole.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/67/Dissolution_sinkhole.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/67/Dissolution_sinkhole.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Rainfall and surface water percolate through joints in the limestone. Dissolved carbonate rock is carried away from the surface, and a small depression gradually forms. On exposed carbonate surfaces, a depression may focus surface drainage, accelerating the dissolution process. Debris carried into the developing sinkhole may plug the outflow, ponding water and creating wetlands.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADissolution_sinkhole.png"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0c/Cover-subsidence_sinkhole.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0c/Cover-subsidence_sinkhole.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Granular sediments spall into secondary openings in the underlying carbonate rocks. A column of overlying sediments settles into the vacated spaces (a process termed \"piping\"). Dissolution and infilling continue, forming a noticeable depression in the land surface. The slow downward erosion eventually forms small surface depressions 1 inch to several feet in depth and diameter.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACover-subsidence_sinkhole.png"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bf/Cover-collapse_sinkhole.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bf/Cover-collapse_sinkhole.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Sediments spall into a cavity. As spalling continues, the cohesive covering sediments form a structural arch. The cavity migrates upward by progressive roof collapse. The cavity eventually breaches the ground surface, creating sudden and dramatic sinkholes.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACover-collapse_sinkhole.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Subsurface_erosion_of_soil_into_karst_conduit_by_water_level_fluctuation.jpg/500px-Subsurface_erosion_of_soil_into_karst_conduit_by_water_level_fluctuation.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Subsurface_erosion_of_soil_into_karst_conduit_by_water_level_fluctuation.jpg/1280px-Subsurface_erosion_of_soil_into_karst_conduit_by_water_level_fluctuation.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Subsurface_erosion_of_soil_into_karst_conduit_by_water_level_fluctuation.jpg/500px-Subsurface_erosion_of_soil_into_karst_conduit_by_water_level_fluctuation.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Man-made activities and land alterations that cause water-level fluctuations accelerate cover-collapse sinkholes","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASubsurface_erosion_of_soil_into_karst_conduit_by_water_level_fluctuation.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/December_Giant_sinkhole_collapse_USGS_1972.jpg/500px-December_Giant_sinkhole_collapse_USGS_1972.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/December_Giant_sinkhole_collapse_USGS_1972.jpg/1280px-December_Giant_sinkhole_collapse_USGS_1972.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/December_Giant_sinkhole_collapse_USGS_1972.jpg/500px-December_Giant_sinkhole_collapse_USGS_1972.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"More than three acres of forest suddenly disappeared into this \"December Giant\" sinkhole in Montevallo, Alabama.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADecember_Giant_sinkhole_collapse_USGS_1972.jpg"}],"sounds":{"sinkhole":[{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/78/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-sinkhole.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/7/78/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-sinkhole.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-sinkhole.wav.mp3"},{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/7/78/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-sinkhole.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-sinkhole.wav.ogg"},{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/af/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Naomi_Persephone_Amethyst_%28NaomiAmethyst%29-sinkhole.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/a/af/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Naomi_Persephone_Amethyst_%28NaomiAmethyst%29-sinkhole.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Naomi_Persepho